.bp
.in 0
.ds
.ig
.ul 1
..
5.0 SYSTEM MAINTENANCE

.ti 3
In this section we will endeavour to do the impossible, that is
explain procedures to protect you against system failures (crashes).
.ti 3
Crashes do not happen.
They are also inevitable.
This is an apparent contradiction but one cannot
go against the laws of probability.
It can and will happen to you.
Therefore backups and system checks are a necessary evil
.ti 3
USE THEM !!!!!!!!!!  REGULARLY !!!!!!!!!

.in 0
.ig
.ul 1
..
5.1 SYSTEM BACKUP

.ti 3
BACKUPs should be taken when the system is quiescent; that is no
users other than the "BACKerUPer" logged in, Batch System
stopped and no processes madly manipulating files.
In other words as quiet and inactive as possible.
.ti 3
The most favoured backup medium is disc both because not all
stations have tapes and discs are bootable where tapes are not.
.ti 3
The system distributed to you has two backup procedures available.

.in 7
.ig
.ul 1
..
5.1.1 "BACKUP" (3 or more discs on system)

.ti 10
On a three disc system it is rare to have a free drive
for the backup disc.
For this reason, usually one or both of the TMP and SPOOL dsics
must be logically UnMOUNTed (see /etc/umount (viii))
from UNIX to allow their safe removal from the drives
before a BACKUP is taken.
.ti 10
The complementary action, logically MOUNTing the discs (/etc/mount (viii)), may be
performed after BACKUP if you wish to continue running
the system although this is not usual.
.ti 10
Having prepared the way, to take a BACKUP of the
disc on drive RKn
onto a scratch or backup disc on drive RKm enter

	"BACKUP RKn RKm"

.ti 10
.ti 10
BACKUP will perform certain checks, copies and
counter checks
as follow (messages advise of BACKUP's current action)

	1) Checks for read permission on RKn
		and read/write permission on RKm
		   notifies an error by

		"either RKn or RKm unacceptable"

	2) ICHECKs RKn

		"icheck /dev/rrkn"

	3) Reads RKn using DD (DD (1))

		"dd if=/dev/rrkn of=/dev/null bs=24b count=203"

	4) Does a SYNC

		"sync"

	5) Copies RKn to RKm using DD
		"dd if=/dev/rrkn of=/dev/rrkm bs=24b count=203"

	6) ICHECKs RKn and RKm

		"icheck /dev/rrkn /dev/rrkm"

	7) Reads RKm

		"dd if=/dev/rrkm of=/dev/null bs=24b count=203"

.ti 10
If at any time errors are noticed in the output of the ICHECKs
(ref. section 4.3.3)
or the DDs have any output at all the BACKUP should be stopped by
hitting the delete or rubout key.
Then call a guru !!!!!!!!!!!.
.ti 10
If all proceeds normally (whats that?) then the backup disc
should be a perfect, readable copy of the disc being backed up.

.in 7
.ig
.ul 1
..
5.1.2 UNIX.RKDF (2 disc systems)

.ti 10
UNIX.RKDF is a stand alone backup routine.
Stand alone means that once loaded and running
it needs no discs mounted at all.
.ti 10
To use this program on small stations (2 discs)
you must first close the system down completely
(ref. section 4.4)
.ti 10
having done this you now BOOT
.ig
.ul 1
..
BUT
in a very special way !!!!!
.ti 10
In the bootstrapping procedure,
just before you finally press the START switch,
reset the switch register to all zero (all switches down).
Then press start as normal.
.ti 10
In general

	  maths and commerce

	 1) Ensure the front panel lock is at POWER position.
	 2) Ensure ENABLE/HALT switch is at HALT.
	 3) Set switch register to 773100 octal.
	 4) Depress and release the LOAD ADDRESS switch.
	 5) Set switch register to 777406 octal.
	 6) Depress and release CONTINUE switch.
	 7) Set switch register to all zeros.
	 8) Put ENABLE/HALT switch to ENABLE.
	 9) Depress and release the START switch.
	10) Enter boot file name in response to "?"

	  library and C.S.U.

	 1) Ensure the front panel lock is at POWER position.
	 2) Ensure ENABLE/HALT switch is at halt.
	 3) Set switch register to 773110 octal.
	 4) Depress and release LOAD ADDRESS switch.
	 5) Put ENABLE/HALT switch to ENABLE.
	 6) Set switch register to all zeros.
	 7) Depress and release the START switch.
	 8) Enter boot file name in response to "?"

	  elec eng and mech eng

	 1) Ensure the front panel lock is at POWER position.
	 2) Ensure ENABLE/HALT switch is at halt.
	 3) Set switch register to 773100 octal.
	 4) Depress and release LOAD ADDRESS switch.
	 5) Put ENABLE/HALT switch to ENABLE.
	 6) Set switch register to all zeros.
	 7) Depress and release the START switch.
	 8) Enter boot file name in response to "?"

.ti 10
When the console prompts with "?"
you enter "UNIX.RKDF"
and wait until asked

	"what function is desired ??"

.ti 10
You may then remove the system disc to make way for backup discs.
.ti 10
If you have never used UNIX.RKDF, typing "H" in answer to the above question
will produce a short summary of available commands.
.ti 10
You then load your discs in the appropriate drives,
enter the necessary command and supply any information requested.
.ti 10
If any output not in plain english is encountered, call a guru.

.in 0
.ig
.ul 1
..
5.2 FILE SYSTEM MAINTENANCE

.ti 3
This section is very brief (I hope !!!)
.ti 3
In order to maintain your system and get the most out of it,
it is imperative that you read the following sections of the UNIX Programmers Manual
and any associated documents.

	ICHECK (viii)
	DCHECK (viii)
	NCHECK (viii)
	CRASH (viii)
	CLRI (viii)
	BOOT (viii)
	UPDATE (viii) (should be killed when patching system disc)


.ti 10
Any future additions to this section will be made at request!

.nx 6omappend
